title: Storage safety
tags: Storage
kind: reference
audience: dev
summary: What survives a crash, a memory bug, a bad disk and a network filesystem — and why reads still wait for writes.

|!Threat |!What protects |!What remains |
|crash, power loss |copy-on-write commits |nothing: the store opens at the last committed transaction |
|a stray write in the daemon's memory |the map is read-only |nothing |
|bad sectors, a disk lying about `fsync` |[[Backups and restore]] |what was written since the last backup |
|a network filesystem |refused at `init` and at load |— |
|a bug in the indexes |they can be rebuilt |wrong answers until `mf repo reindex` |
|a long read transaction |none outlives its request |— |

!! Crash and power loss

A copy-on-write B+tree never modifies a live page. A commit writes the new pages
elsewhere, syncs them, then flips a small header to the new root (there are two
headers, used alternately). A crash before the flip leaves the previous header in
force: the store opens at the last committed transaction, with no recovery log to
replay.

!! Our own memory bugs

The map is opened ''read-only'' (no `MDB_WRITEMAP`): writes go through the
library's system calls, so a stray write in the process cannot reach the file.

!! A damaged disk

LMDB has no page checksums. A damaged page makes part of the store unreadable — all
of it, for a page near the root — or silently wrong. The defence is a backup, taken
weekly by default (`auto-backup-days`) and checked when taken
([[Backups and restore]]); whether to trade for a store with checksums is an open
question ([[Storage open questions]]).

!! Network filesystems

LMDB does not support NFS, SMB, SSHFS and the like. A `.metafolder/` on one is
refused at `init` and at load, its filesystem type read from `/proc/self/mountinfo`:
NFS, CIFS/SMB, 9p, the cluster filesystems, and FUSE only for its remote kinds (a
local drive through `fuse.ntfs-3g` is not refused). The files may stay on the share:
only `.metafolder/` must be local — an external one ([[Network and special filesystems]]).
Where the mount table cannot be read, nothing is refused.

!! Indexes

A bug in the indexes never touches the primary data, and they can always be rebuilt
from it ([[Checking and reindexing a repository]]).

!! Long read transactions

A read transaction pins the pages it sees, so a long one makes the file grow. A read
snapshot borrows the store behind the repository's lock, so none outlives the request
holding it; no static check enforces more than that.

!! Reads wait for writes

LMDB lets readers work past a writer, but here a query still takes the repository's
lock, as every read did before: its snapshot borrows the store behind that lock. So a
read waits for a long write. Letting reads open their snapshot without the lock needs
the map's growth to exclude open readers — LMDB resizes only with no transaction open
in the process ([[Writes and atomicity]]). ''Deferred by decision'' (September 2026):
operations stay sequential for now.
